2026-10-13 — K01: original replay blocked; the EvoCC-only timeout is a slowdown.

This is a diagnosis record, not the unchanged sched_ext replay and not K13.
No kernel, test, assertion, budget or lane is changed.

Replay status: NOT_RUN. The pinned 7,008-unit composition (EvoCC Image
18b36fee4bb0265719f59df8db1da7098374df3c5e245071dc8f0662ea6a7032, GCC
22a87df276887cc6454053dee56ea96742ab9cf24043b00fd1a21accf93a0fa6), its
config 95853321..., the older retained pairs (f2d59e05.../8fa43fb4...,
62c29ae4.../13bd4d0b...), the matched runtime disks and the sched_ext
payloads were under .build/k7-coverage, retired on October 7. A search of
every Image file under .build, .build/case and .evidence finds none of these
hashes. The two current Images, GCC virtconfig b6f206dea584... and EvoCC
K16/K17 add1c4874e28..., do not enable CONFIG_SCHED_CLASS_EXT. This slice
may not build a kernel. The hotplug -> reload_loop sequence and the complete
23-test program therefore wait for restored inputs or an owner-approved new
pair, as the October 11 preflight already records.

Retained event evidence: the unchanged full-program pairs on tcg-cortex-a72
(.evidence/kselftest-full/RUN/{reference,subject}/sched_ext/runner/, plus
the deleted September 28 captures summarized in qemu-cache-maintenance.out).
Reference times are the guest power-off times:
  run                            GCC reference    EvoCC subject
  20260922T033617Z               23/23, 48.8 s    TIMEOUT, select_cpu_dfl
  seven-differences-serial-0923  23/23, 49.0 s    NULL fault, reload_loop
  sched-blocked-stacks-0923      not run          TIMEOUT, init_enable_count
  20260927T223104Z               23/23, 56.9 s    TIMEOUT, init_enable_count
  qemu-cache-maintenance 0928    23/23, twice     TIMEOUT, reload_loop;
                                                  candidate: TIMEOUT, prog_run
The timeout point moves between runs of the same old subject Image
f2d59e05...: reload_loop (test 16), init_enable_count (test 9) and
prog_run (test 15). A deterministic hang would stop at one place.

Not a hang. The three retained EvoCC timeout captures contain no RCU stall,
soft lockup, hung task or oops report. In sched-blocked-stacks-20260923
(console level 7, PID 1 requests SysRq show-state at the limit):
  21.4 s   hotplug ends
  80.69 s  init_enable_count attaches (global): 59.3 s after hotplug
  81.72 s  detaches after the 1024 pre-forked children exit
  82.38 s  second global attach/detach ends
  140.93 s partial run attaches: 58.6 s after 82.38 s
  141.20 s show-state: the runner (pid 130) waits in wait4; its children
           2199-2207 sleep in hrtimer_nanosleep; no task waits in D state.
Both phases run open/load plus 1024 forks and take about 59 s each. The
test was progressing at the limit. It needed two such phases.

Not explained by the MT-TCG defect. The September 28 cache-maintenance QEMU
removes all reduced publication failures, yet the same old subject still
times out (prog_run). The show-state above has only ordinary sleeping and
waiting tasks. The defect remains the open candidate for the NULL fault in
reload_loop, not for the timeout (k01b-qemu-mttcg-disposition.out).

A slowdown: instruction counts on the current pair (a proxy, not sched_ext).
fork-window.c repeats init_enable_count's pre-attach pattern: 1024 fork()
children that exit, then waitpid for each. A 10M-iteration user loop is the
control. GCC 14.2 static, sealed image; identical initramfs on both kernels.
  Fixed instruction clock, ST-TCG (-icount shift=0,sleep=off; 1 ns = one
  executed instruction on any vCPU, plus skipped all-idle time):
                          GCC kernel        EvoCC kernel      ratio
    user-loop control     60.3 M / 63.3 M   60.9 M / 71.8 M   1.01-1.13
    1024 fork+exit+wait   241-247 M         2,224-2,252 M     9.1-9.3
  MT-TCG (locked lane) with the instruction-window plugin, which counts
  executed instructions on all vCPUs between two HINT markers:
    user-loop control     60.4 M / 60.5 M   61.0 M / 65.0 M   1.0-1.1
    1024 fork+exit+wait   269-281 M         2,720-2,891 M     9.9-10.3
    same, children sleep  287-316 M         2,712-2,876 M     9.1-9.5
  The counts do not depend on host load. Three rounds per program agree
  within 10%.
A per-block plugin attributes 92-97% of three fork windows to functions:
  EvoCC copy_pte_range  2,063 M; GCC's whole copy_page_range is 17.3 M.
    EvoCC's copy_pte_range symbol is 1,329,200 bytes (0x144830) and runs
    630 distinct hot blocks; GCC inlines this path into a 9,000-byte
    copy_page_range. The EvoCC fork page-table copy path costs about 120x.
  PTE zap at exit: EvoCC zap_pte_range + zap_present_ptes + unmap_page_range
    1,037 M (34 KiB + 33 KiB + 10 KiB of code); GCC's 4.7 KiB
    unmap_page_range, with this path inlined, 48.8 M.
  ktime_get + ktime_get_update_offsets_now: 1,060 M vs 63 M; one ktime_get
    block alone retires 565 M, consistent with sequence-count spinning.
  queued_spin_lock_slowpath: 346 M vs 28 M.
EvoCC total 9,040 M vs GCC 884 M (10.2x). At the logged load the EvoCC
rounds take 26-36 s of guest time against 5-14 s for GCC; the September
59-second phases (open, load and 1024 forks of the larger runner) fit this.

Conclusion: the EvoCC-only full-program timeout is a slowdown. It is not a
hang, and the MT-TCG defect does not cause it. The retained September runs
spend about 59 s in each init_enable_count fork phase, and host load moves
the stopping point within the fixed 120-second budget. On the current pair
the same fork pattern runs about ten times more kernel instructions on
EvoCC, concentrated in compiler output (a 1.3 MB copy_pte_range and
un-inlined PTE loops). This points to a code-generation performance cause
of the K02/K03 kind, not an emulator or test cause. The counts come from
the current compiler (820bfa29) on the virtconfig pair; the September
compositions are retired and were not measured.

What remains for K01:
  - Unchanged paired replay of hotplug -> reload_loop and the 23-test program
    on a restored or owner-approved sched_ext pair. A subject PASS within the
    unchanged 120 s budget likely needs the fork-path code size repaired.
  - The NULL fault's cause. The MT-TCG stale-block defect is the candidate;
    no causal link is proven.

Tooling notes. The first plugin attempt used -accel tcg,thread=single with a
plugin: QEMU 11.1.2 aborts at once with "target/arm/helper.c:10170:
arm_mmu_idx_el: code should not be reached" (both Images, QEMU_EXIT_134).
That is why the plugin runs use MT-TCG. qemu_plugin_outs prints only with
"-d plugin"; one MT run without it has no counts and is kept as such.

